QR code generator: qrencode (the console menu's "Open on your phone" shows the web app URL as a QR)
Ship: yes
Project: libqrencode / qrencode, https://fukuchi.org/works/qrencode/
Release: 4.1.1-2build1, the Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute) packages qrencode and libqrencode4
License: LGPL-2.1-or-later (LICENSE, shipped here); the Debian packaging is public domain
Packaged: not built here - mkimage.sh unpacks the two .debs into ~/.cache/1bit-os/tools/x on first use
          (the same route as Dropbear and mtools), copies /usr/bin/qrencode to /bin/qrencode and
          resolves what it links with ldd under that tree (libqrencode.so.4 from this package, plus
          libpng16, libz, libm and libc, which the image already carries or takes from Ubuntu).
          Each package's own /usr/share/doc/<pkg>/copyright is installed in the image beside the
          others. They are unpacked, not dpkg-installed, so `dpkg -S` cannot attribute their files and
          /usr/share/licenses/1bit-os/packages.txt does not name them - the same as Dropbear and
          mtools, whose copyright files travel the same way.
Why it ships: the physical console cannot be clicked (JetKVM video, a monitor), so the owner asked for
          a code a phone can scan; qrencode is the smallest tool that renders one in the terminal
          without a graphics stack. It is run only when the menu's QR screen is opened.
Source: the .debs are the upstream 4.1.1 source as packaged by Debian/Ubuntu; upstream tarball
          https://fukuchi.org/works/qrencode/qrencode-4.1.1.tar.gz, Debian packaging
          https://salsa.debian.org/debian/qrencode . Nothing is patched: the image carries the
          packaged binaries unmodified.
